course: dictionary
lesson: 12015
title: Chewy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe how food feels in your mouth. Chewy is one of them. Something chewy is soft but tough, so you have to chew it for a long time before you can swallow it. «The toffee was chewy and stuck to my back teeth.» The word chewy tells you the toffee needed lots of chewing.
explain: Context gives you clues. «The cookies were chewy in the middle and crisp at the edges.» Crisp means hard and crunchy, so chewy must be the opposite: soft but needing lots of chewing. Ask yourself: what would I have to do with this food? If the answer is chew, chewy is the word.
explain: Chewy is an adjective, so it describes a noun. It can change to chewier and chewiest when you compare things: «This bread is chewier than yesterday's loaf.» «The black sweet was the chewiest in the bag.»
fact: Chewy means soft but tough, so it needs a lot of chewing.
fact: «Chewy sweets» and «chewy meat» are examples. The food is not crunchy and not runny.
fact: Chewy can be an adjective: chewy, chewier, chewiest.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, «chewy» is informal slang for chewing gum: «Got any chewy?» means «Have you got any gum?»
fact: Sticky chewy sweets can stay on your teeth for a long time, so dentists advise eating them only now and then.
exercise: «The toffee was chewy, so Sam chewed for a long time.» What does chewy mean? | *Soft but tough, needing lots of chewing | Runny like soup | Cold and icy | Crunchy and loud
exercise: Which food is most likely to be chewy? | A glass of water | A bowl of cold soup | *A sticky caramel that stretches when you pull it | A crunchy apple
exercise: An Australian friend asks, «Got any chewy?» What does she want? | *Some chewing gum | A drink of water | A chewy cookie to eat | Some fruit
test: «The black sweet was the chewiest in the bag.» Which word is the comparing form of chewy? | chewer | chewing | chewed | *chewiest
test: Why might a dentist warn about sticky chewy sweets? | *They can stick to teeth and harm them | They make teeth shine | They are too crunchy to bite | They help teeth grow stronger
exam: Ellie says chewy means crunchy. Which answer is right? | *Chewy means soft but tough, so Ellie is wrong | Chewy means crunchy, so Ellie is right | Chewy means runny, so Ellie is right | Chewy means cold, so Ellie is wrong
